A||||5||ggriner||Headlight motor only works one way?||||1||
Z||000000||ggriner||07-19-2001||02:20 PM||||I have an 86 Fiero that just recently has had a headlight motor problem.  The right headlight will not rise when turned on.  The light itself works.  If I rotate the circular knob on top manually,  it rises up and stays in position.  When I turn the lights off it drops back down and off.  It's obviously not the gears inside.  Any ideas on what it needs to make it function?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||07-19-2001||02:27 PM||||It's the relay next to the headlight motor or the down side limit switch in the motor.<BR>It thinks the light is already up. When you turn it up it switches the other limit switch and then it goes down. It might also be as simple as removing the connectors and cleaning the contacts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero_24||07-19-2001||03:16 PM||||This might work.  On my 86 Coupe I had to hold the headlamp switch in just barely and when the light popped up I would push it in the rest of the way.  Try it, I never solved the problem and I replaced everything trying to figure it out.  My guess is the ground wire to it.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||9
Z||000003||ggriner||07-20-2001||11:39 AM||||Thanks for the advice, I noticed that the unit next to the actual headlight (don't know technical names, but the part where all the wires go) was hanging loose on that side.  I had just changed the headlight bulb a few days earlier and had added some coolant the same day the unit quit working.  I unscrewed the panel on the side of the motor and could see several spring type switches which I'm assuming are the problem.  I'll dig into it this weekend and if it's a mechanical problem (as opposed to electronic) I'm sure I can figure it out.  I'm in my mid 50's and would never have dreamed when I was 17 (back in the 60's) and working on my 53 Ford that there would be such a fantastic resource where you can get such usable information almost instantly.  I appreciate everyone taking time to help with these little problems that would cost an arm and a leg to have fixed at a dealer.  I own an 84 SE, 86 2M6, 87 SE and an 88 GT with the hopes of finding an 85 to complete the set soon.  Again, Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||07-20-2001||11:55 AM||||I have extra 84-86 style motors, one for each side, along with the relays if it turns out you need one.  If so, just drop me an e-mail.  I am converting my 85 GT over to the 87/88 style motors.<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||07-20-2001||11:07 PM||||ggriner, welcome to the forum. I know how ya feel,I'm 52. Flathead Fords and a straight 8 Packard are what I started out on many moons ago. Information,entertainment; discovery channel doesn't hold a candle to these people. They know stuff cause they've done it and are glad to share. Welcome aboard<BR>Don & Jane<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 2.5<BR>not slow not fast-just halfast||||reg||1
